2026-07-19T19:22:49+1000 [INFO] drawio-agent: llm_input | step=llm_input level=info msg="LLM request" ts=2026-07-19T09:22:49.243095+00:00 req_id=d2d6a5ffdb04 details={'system_prompt': 'You are generating or modifying a cloud architecture diagram in mxGraph/draw.io XML. Place every service inside the correct container via parent= — never by coordinates alone.\n\n**Service icon pixels** come from Draw.io MCP via each leaf\'s `shape_type` attribute. On service leaves use a placeholder style only — do **not** invent stencil libraries (`resIcon`, `resourceIcon`, `mxgraph.gcp2`, `image=img/lib/azure2`) on leaves; the server merges official icons after generation. Groups/containers keep LLM-owned styles and must not use `shape_type`.\n\n## Create vs Edit Modes (CRITICAL — do not break existing diagrams)\n\nBefore placing: detect existing provider(s) and container ids/labels and reuse them.\n\n- **Create (new diagram):** apply the full craft below (pitch, wrap, naming, edge anchors, page size).\n- **Edit (modify existing):** keep existing cells\' parent, coordinates, and sizes unless the user asked to fix layout. Place **new** leaves only in the next free grid slot (wrap after 4). Grow the immediate parent (and ancestors) as needed. Do **not** reshuffle unrelated icons "to improve spacing."\n- **Explicit layout-fix request** (user says fix crowding / clean up / re-layout): re-grid **only inside the named container(s)** using the service grid; preserve ids/parents where possible; still avoid rewriting the whole canvas hierarchy.\n\n## Canonical Container Hierarchy — STRICT NESTING (CRITICAL)\n\nEvery internal box must be **visually and structurally wrapped** inside its outer layer. Nesting is done with `parent="<outer-id>"` and **relative** child geometry — never by drawing boxes side-by-side on the root canvas that only look nested.\n\n```\n[Cloud Provider Boundary]              ← OUTER WRAPPER (only this + background + optional Users sit on parent="1")\n ├─ [Region]                           ← parent = Cloud id\n │   └─ [Virtual Network] (VPC|VNet)   ← parent = Region id\n │       ├─ [Load Balancer / Gateway]  ← parent = VPC id (sibling of Zones)\n │       ├─ [Zone] × N                 ← parent = VPC id\n │       │   ├─ [Public Subnet]        ← parent = Zone id\n │       │   ├─ [Private App Subnet]   ← parent = Zone id\n │       │   └─ [Private Data Subnet]  ← parent = Zone id\n │       │       └─ [service icons]    ← parent = Subnet id\n │       ├─ [Security Group]           ← parent = VPC id  (NO edges)\n │       ├─ [Monitoring Group]         ← parent = VPC id  (NO edges)\n │       ├─ [Integration Group]        ← parent = VPC id  (NO edges)\n │       └─ [Management Group]         ← parent = VPC id  (NO edges)\n └─ [Edge/Global Layer]                ← parent = Cloud id (sibling of Region, still INSIDE Cloud)\n     └─ DNS / CDN / WAF / static       ← parent = Edge layer id\n```\n\n**Outer-layer wrap rules:**\n1. Create the Cloud boundary first. Its `parent="1"`. Size it large enough to contain Region + Edge + padding.\n2. Region is **inside** Cloud (`parent=<cloud-id>`). VPC is **inside** Region. Zones/subnets/groups are **inside** VPC. Services are **inside** their subnet or supporting group.\n3. **FORBIDDEN:** Region, VPC, Zone, Subnet, Security/Monitoring/Integration/Management, or Edge as `parent="1"` siblings floating beside the Cloud box. If it belongs in the hierarchy, it must be a child of the layer above it.\n4. **FORBIDDEN:** placing an inner box with absolute canvas coordinates while `parent="1"` so it only overlaps the outer box visually. Overlap ≠ containment.\n5. Outer containers must be **larger than** the bounding box of all their children. Use **tight** shrink-wrap: pad **20–28px** sides/bottom, **≥48px** top for title — no huge empty regions. Grow Cloud/Region/VPC/Zone/Subnet until every child fits; do not leave oversized empty boxes.\n6. Optional **Users / Internet / Client** icons: if used, they are **always** `parent="1"` (left of Cloud) — external actors represent people/systems outside the cloud boundary, not a service to be classified or nested. Everything else cloud-related stays wrapped inside Cloud.\n7. **FORBIDDEN:** an external-actor icon (Users / Internet / Client / mobile / on-prem / IoT device) as a child of Cloud, Region, VPC, or any other container. It never has a classification decision — it is not a service.\n\n## Leaf Footprint — UNDER-ICON LABELS (CRITICAL)\n\nService leaves use `verticalLabelPosition=bottom` — the **label draws below** the icon box. Parent sizing must account for that extra band, not only the 78×78 icon geometry.\n\n- Icon size: 78×78 main, 65×65 secondary\n- Under-label band: leave **≥ 32–40px** clear space below the icon inside the parent (labels must not kiss the subnet border or the next row)\n- Labels may be **wider than the icon** (assume up to ~120px centered) → ~21px overhang each side. Adjacent icons must use enough pitch so overhangs do not collide\n- Parent width must include: last icon `x + max(icon.width, ~labelWidth) + pad` — not merely `x + 78`\n- Parent height must include: `child.y + child.height + labelBand + pad`\n- Self-check: every child\'s **icon and under-label** fit insi... (truncated, 27382 total chars)', 'human_content': 'Platform: AWS\nRequest:\nGenerate an AWS multi-region, multi-account disaster-recovery architecture for an insurance claims-processing platform, with the following requirements:\nRegion 1 (us-east-1) — Primary, Account: prod-primary\n\t○ VPC (10.0.0.0/16) containing 3 Availability Zones, each with a public subnet and a private subnet\n\t○ Public subnets: NAT Gateway + Application Load Balancer (internet-facing) spanning all 3 AZs\n\t○ Private subnets: ECS Fargate cluster running 3 microservices (Claims API, Underwriting Engine, Document OCR service), each in its own private subnet per AZ\n\t○ RDS PostgreSQL Multi-AZ cluster (primary + 2 standby replicas) in dedicated DB subnet group, isolated from app subnets\n\t○ ElastiCache Redis cluster (3 nodes) for session caching, in private subnet\n\t○ S3 bucket for claims documents, with a VPC Gateway Endpoint (not internet-routed)\n\t○ SQS queue + Lambda consumer for async claims-notification processing, triggered by S3 event notifications\n\t○ API Gateway in front of the ALB for external partner integrations, with a WAF attached\nRegion 2 (us-west-2) — DR/Standby, Account: prod-dr\n\t○ Mirrored VPC (10.1.0.0/16) with the same 3-AZ structure but only 1 AZ active (warm standby), ECS services scaled to 0\n\t○ RDS read replica (cross-region) replicating from the primary cluster\n\t○ S3 Cross-Region Replication target bucket\nCross-cutting components:\n\t○ Transit Gateway connecting both VPCs across accounts and regions\n\t○ Route 53 with health-check-based failover routing between the two regional ALBs\n\t○ AWS Backup vault (in a 3rd account: prod-backup) pulling scheduled snapshots from both RDS clusters and S3 buckets, connected via cross-account IAM roles (draw as dashed trust-relationship lines, not data-flow lines)\n\t○ CloudWatch + SNS alerting pipeline monitoring all services, fanning out to a Lambda that posts to a Slack webhook\n\t○ AWS Secrets Manager storing DB credentials, referenced by both the primary ECS tasks and the Fargate DR tasks\nDraw all three AWS accounts as separate top-level bounding containers (grouped by account, not just region), with VPCs nested inside accounts, subnets nested inside VPCs, and AZs shown as either horizontal swim-lanes or nested containers around the subnet pairs. Label every subnet with its CIDR block. Distinguish data-flow edges (solid) from control/IAM-trust edges (dashed) from replication edges (dotted, with a direction arrow showing replica lag direction). Use official AWS4 stencils throughout — no generic rectangles for any AWS service.'}
