m app subnets\n\t○ ElastiCache Redis cluster (3 nodes) for session caching, in private subnet\n\t○ S3 bucket for claims documents, with a VPC Gateway Endpoint (not internet-routed)\n\t○ SQS queue + Lambda consumer for async claims-notification processing, triggered by S3 event notifications\n\t○ API Gateway in front of the ALB for external partner integrations, with a WAF attached\nRegion 2 (us-west-2) — DR/Standby, Account: prod-dr\n\t○ Mirrored VPC (10.1.0.0/16) with the same 3-AZ structure but only 1 AZ active (warm standby), ECS services scaled to 0\n\t○ RDS read replica (cross-region) replicating from the primary cluster\n\t○ S3 Cross-Region Replication target bucket\nCross-cutting components:\n\t○ Transit Gateway connecting both VPCs across accounts and regions\n\t○ Route 53 with health-check-based failover routing between the two regional ALBs\n\t○ AWS Backup vault (in a 3rd account: prod-backup) pulling scheduled snapshots from both RDS clusters and S3 buckets, connected via cross-account IAM roles (draw as dashed trust-relationship lines, not data-flow lines)\n\t○ CloudWatch + SNS alerting pipeline monitoring all services, fanning out to a Lambda that posts to a Slack webhook\n\t○ AWS Secrets Manager storing DB credentials, referenced by both the primary ECS tasks and the Fargate DR tasks\nDraw all three AWS accounts as separate top-level bounding containers (grouped by account, not just region), with VPCs nested inside accounts, subnets nested inside VPCs, and AZs shown as either horizontal swim-lanes or nested containers around the subnet pairs. Label every subnet with its CIDR block. Distinguish data-flow edges (solid) from control/IAM-trust edges (dashed) from replication edges (dotted, with a direction arrow showing replica lag direction). Use official AWS4 stencils throughout — no generic rectangles for any AWS service.'}
